I'm Harold Varmus I'm a cancer biologist  and I'm the president of Memorial  sloan-kettering Cancer Center I'd like  to talk to you a little bit about how  somebody becomes a scientist many people  think that scientists are born and  they're born with with special  properties they listen to Mozart in the  crib they they excel at math even in  grade school they join the Chess Club  they don't do athletics they they end up  being victors and science fairs before  they get to college and they enter a  track that leads to advanced degrees in  science and engineering and of course  this sometimes happens and reinforces  the popular image but there are lots of  ways to become a scientist and it's  important at a time when the u.s. is  losing numbers of scientists and not  training enough to sustain our  leadership and industry and and and the  sciences to think about other ways in  which people do become scientists I had  myself an unusual route to being a  scientist I'd like to spend a little bit  of time telling you about what that  route was like so I was a good but not  exceptional student in public high  schools in Freeport New York on Long  Island I didn't have strong science  teachers I didn't show any particular  interest in science I didn't spend my  afternoons with a chemistry set I've  spent more than playing tennis and going  to the beach or reading novels than I  did attempting to win science fairs my  father was a doctor and I expected to  follow him into medicine and after high  school went off to Amherst College is a  pre-medical student and there at Amherst  placed before me was a very rich table  of intellectual opportunities and  physics and philosophy  literature in history and I found myself  spending most of my time running the  college newspaper and doing a thesis on  Charles Dickens and spending time with  my literary friends I continue to think  about medical school but didn't do  particularly well in chemistry and  decided finally to go off to graduate  school in English literature where I  spent a year before deciding again to  think about medical school and after  being rejected by one Harvard I chose to  go to another Columbia and found myself  engrossed in sequence in psychiatry and  then internal medicine never paying much  attention to serious laboratory research  but interested in other serious forms of  research in the clinic but not really  engaged in any of those forms I did  house staff training and then at the age  of 28 the Vietnam War required that I  make a decision about a way to serve my  country without serving a war I didn't  believe in I was able to do that thanks  to a program that allowed those of us  who were dissenters from the war to work  at the National Institutes of Health and  here for reasons that beyond this  discussion I was plunged into work on  the molecular biology of E coli the way  in which bacteria regulate expression of  a set of genes called the lac operon  this exposed me for the first time to  the excitement of laboratory research I  was a lot older than most students who  write to me today to work on an Intel  project in my laboratory at  sloan-kettering but nevertheless I still  had the same kind of enthusiasm I see in  those high school students for  understanding how a bacterial organism  controls its genes molecular biology was  in its early phases the idea of using  bacteria or other simple organized  as model organisms for understanding  higher levels of regulation in complex  organisms was still again at its early  stages and this was an exhilarating time  but it's not something I had been  trained for and I wanted to get back to  something that was more medically  related which I did when I discovered  through coursework at the NIH that that  there was a new excitement about viruses  very small packets of genes that were  able to turn a normal cell into a cancer  cell cancer was an important and is an  important human disease I was interested  in trying to understand with the new  tools of molecular biology and at that  point the age of 30 I came to the  University of California San Francisco  to begin what proved to be over two  decades of work on how cancer viruses  grow and how they turn a normal cell  into a cancer cell the reason I tell  this story is to illustrate that there  are lots of ways to become a scientist  and that in the u.s. one of the great  advantages we have is that we don't  require that people make career  decisions at an early age we allow we  even encourage what I would call a  prolonged adolescence this is something  to keep in mind as you progress through  high school and college there is still  time to make decisions about the way  you're going to conduct your careers  moreover it's important to remember that  not every scientist is a math whiz as a  grade school student that there are lots  of ways to become a scientist and lots  of ways to be a scientist in fact I  would argue that that while science can  involve experimental procedures and  analysis of classical experiments there  are lots of other ways to think like a  scientist even in fields that are  outside of what we call the conventional  natural sciences so thinking like a  scientist means thinking about evidence  gathering it evaluating it and  interpreting it in a way that helps to  make sense of the world in which we live  we need more of this in our  world and it's with an effort to  understand that there are lots of paths  to developing a scientific outlook that  can influence your career and help  strengthen our society that I offer this  brief story of an unconventional way to  become a scientist  you
 

